108
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:00,160
and that's going to take up the rest of the podcast.

109
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:01,600
So let's get started, shall we?

110
00:06:01,600 --> 00:06:05,840
Scout MasterChip in seven minutes or less.

111
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,120
Here's an interesting thought.

112
00:06:15,120 --> 00:06:16,880
Scouting has a scale.

113
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:21,760
It was designed to work at a specific scale.

114
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:23,440
To understand what that scale is,

115
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:25,040
we don't have to go any further

116
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:28,560
than reading what the founder of Scouting had to say about it.

117
00:06:28,560 --> 00:06:32,400
And here is a quote from Baden Powell,

118
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:34,800
from an article that he wrote for The Scouter,

119
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:37,440
which was the period article published

120
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:40,080
by the Scouts Association in Great Britain.

121
00:06:40,080 --> 00:06:41,920
He wrote this in the 1920s

122
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:43,760
and let me just read it to you here,

123
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:45,840
As the camping season is now upon us.

124
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:48,800
I may say that one or two of the camps

125
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:50,560
which I have already seen

126
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:53,760
have been unfortunately on the wrong lines,

127
00:06:53,760 --> 00:06:56,000
though others were very satisfactory.

128
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:59,200
I strongly advise small camps

129
00:06:59,200 --> 00:07:01,200
of about a half a dozen patrols,

130
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:02,880
each patrol in a separate tent

131
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:04,240
and on a separate ground,

132
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:06,719
as suggested in Scouting for Boys.

133
00:07:06,719 --> 00:07:09,039
So the Scouts do not feel themselves

134
00:07:09,039 --> 00:07:11,039
to be part of a big herd

135
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:14,320
but members of independent, responsible units.

136
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:16,960
Large camps prevent Scout work

137
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:19,920
and necessitate military training,

138
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:22,640
and one which I visited the other day,

139
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:24,640
though exceedingly well carried out

140
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:26,400
as a bit of army organization,

141
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:28,640
appealed to me very little

142
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:30,720
because it was not only entirely

143
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:32,240
on military lines

144
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:34,880
but the patrols- the essence of our system-

145
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:38,720
were broken up to fit the members into the tents.

146
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:39,840
He went on to say: patrols

147
00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:42,960
should be kept intact under all circumstances.

148
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:44,640
If more than six or seven patrols

149
00:07:44,640 --> 00:07:45,600
are out at the same time,

150
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:47,440
they should preferably be divided

151
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:49,120
into two camps located,

152
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:52,320
say, two miles or more apart.

153
00:07:52,320 --> 00:07:54,720
So Scouting has a scale

154
00:07:54,720 --> 00:07:55,760
and for our purposes here

155
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:57,520
we're going to we're going to look at

156
00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:59,360
two different scales

157
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:01,760
that Ben Powell pointed out

158
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:02,880
in this article:

159
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:04,640
the patrol and the herd.

160
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:06,320
Our target, the place where

161
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:08,240
everything happens in Scouting,

162
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:10,800
is the Scout and the smaller group

163
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:11,840
or his patrol.

164
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:12,800
As Scout Masters,

165
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:14,480
our most important mandate

166
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:15,920
is keeping the program

167
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:17,440
targeted at building

168
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:18,720
and supporting patrols,

169
00:08:18,720 --> 00:08:21,680
because patrols are the scale of Scouting.

170
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:23,680
Herds are too big,

171
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:25,120
they're too large for Scouting

172
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:26,400
to work effectively.

173
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:27,920
The real work happens

174
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:30,400
when Scouts are working in a patrol

175
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:32,320
and they develop mutual respect

176
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:32,960
for each other

177
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:34,320
and foster cooperation

178
00:08:34,320 --> 00:08:35,919
within a group of equals.

179
00:08:35,919 --> 00:08:37,600
and this cooperation,

180
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:39,120
this small group, is where

181
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:40,880
the really radical ideas

182
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:42,000
like self-government

183
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,280
through the patrol method

184
00:08:43,280 --> 00:08:44,320
take place.

185
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:46,880
A patrol is this kind of

186
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:48,720
laboratory of leadership

187
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:50,080
and personal development.

188
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:51,280
Ben Powell called it

189
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:52,480
the character school

190
00:08:52,480 --> 00:08:53,600
for the individual.

191
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:54,720
Everybody in a patrol

192
00:08:54,720 --> 00:08:56,800
has some kind of responsibility.

193
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:58,240
Everybody in a patrol

194
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:00,960
has a part in the decision-making process

195
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:02,800
and in the direction of the patrol.

196
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:05,280
The reason that we're focused on patrols

197
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:06,160
is because

198
00:09:06,160 --> 00:09:08,160
those things just don't happen

199
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:09,360
when we're treating Scouts

200
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:10,880
as a big giant herd.

201
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,160
A Scout in a patrol

202
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:12,880
of you know

203
00:09:12,880 --> 00:09:14,480
five to eight guys

204
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:15,600
doesn't get lost

205
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:16,560
like he would

206
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:17,839
in a big herd

207
00:09:17,839 --> 00:09:19,680
of 30 or 40 Scouts,

208
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:20,880
where he's just another face

209
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:21,600
in the crowd.

210
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:23,280
Within the patrol,

211
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:24,959
Scouts act on this notion

212
00:09:24,959 --> 00:09:26,719
of reciprocity.

213
00:09:26,719 --> 00:09:28,319
Cooperation, with all its

214
00:09:28,319 --> 00:09:29,439
troubles and triumphs

215
00:09:29,439 --> 00:09:30,720
and difficulties,

216
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:32,160
enables each Scout

217
00:09:32,160 --> 00:09:34,400
to discover more about themselves

218
00:09:34,400 --> 00:09:36,079
and to integrate the concepts

219
00:09:36,080 --> 00:09:37,840
of the oath and law

220
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:38,960
into their character

221
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:40,720
in a very real way.

222
00:09:40,720 --> 00:09:42,720
When we have real self-government,

223
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:44,080
the Scout Oath and Law

224
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:46,000
are not just a bunch of concepts

225
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,360
that are preached by adults.

226
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:48,720
They're very meaningful,

227
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:50,000
They're very useful,

228
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:51,280
They become lively,

229
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:53,120
motivating concepts,

230
00:09:53,120 --> 00:09:54,080
because that's the only way

231
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:54,960
you're going to be able to get

232
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:56,080
anything done

233
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:57,120
in a patrol.

234
00:09:57,120 --> 00:09:57,920
You have to do it

235
00:09:57,920 --> 00:09:59,600
by the Scout Oath and Law.

236
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:00,560
We want to foster

237
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:02,160
this cooperative independence

238
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:03,760
using the patrol method.

239
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:05,280
So the central importance

240
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:07,040
of that prioritizes

241
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:08,000
everything else

242
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:08,880
about Scouting.

243
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:10,800
It's the scale of Scouting.

244
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:11,600
If we're trying to work

245
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:12,720
with Scouts in a herd

246
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:15,040
of 20, 30, 40 Scouts

247
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:16,400
it's not going to be

248
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:17,600
as effective

249
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:19,839
at achieving the aims of Scouting

250
00:10:19,839 --> 00:10:21,680
as it is when they are working

251
00:10:21,680 --> 00:10:23,839
with a group of peers

252
00:10:23,839 --> 00:10:25,439
in that group of you know

253
00:10:25,439 --> 00:10:26,880
five or eight Scouts

254
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:27,920
in a patrol.

255
00:10:27,920 --> 00:10:28,880
So when we're camping,

256
00:10:28,880 --> 00:10:29,760
patrols should have

257
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:30,800
their own spot.

258
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:32,560
and I know that

259
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:34,480
it's a little bit of an eyebrow razor

260
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:36,080
when you read that.

261
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:37,440
Baden Powell says

262
00:10:37,440 --> 00:10:39,040
if more than six or seven patrols

263
00:10:39,040 --> 00:10:39,920
are out at the same time,

264
00:10:39,920 --> 00:10:41,520
they should be divided into two camps

265
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:43,280
like two miles or more apart.

266
00:10:43,280 --> 00:10:44,640
But he noted the difference

267
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:46,240
between the military precision

268
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:47,840
of having an even number

269
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:49,280
of tents and dividing the Scouts

270
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:50,080
up into the tents

271
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:51,600
and ignoring the patrols.

272
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:53,600
and it's a really apt illustration

273
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:54,400
of the idea

274
